1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,020
Listen to a part of talk on astronomy.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:03,740
Thank you. It's great to see

3
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:08,010
so many of you interested in this series on "Survival in Outer Space."

4
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:12,090
Please excuse the cameras, we're being videotaped for the local TV stations.

5
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:17,190
Tonight I'm going to talk about the most basic aspect of survival --- the space suit.

6
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:19,320
When most of you imagine an astronaut

7
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:22,524
that's probably the first thing that comes to mind, right?

8
00:00:22,524 --> 00:00:27,230
Well, without spaces suits, it would not be possible for us to survive in space.

9
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:33,313
For example, outer space is a vacuum --- there's no gravity or air pressure;

10
00:00:33,313 --> 00:00:36,120
without protection, a body would explode.

11
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:40,225
What's more, we'd cook in the sun or freeze in the shade ---

12
00:00:40,225 --> 00:00:46,040
with temperatures ranging from a toasty 300 degrees above to a cool 300 degrees below zero Fahrenheit.

13
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,723
The space suit that NASA has developed is truly a marvel.

14
00:00:49,723 --> 00:00:57,090
This photo enlargement here is a life-size image of an actual space suit worn by astronauts on the last space shuttle mission.

15
00:00:57,090 --> 00:01:02,296
This part is the torso --- it's made of seven extremely durable layers.

16
00:01:02,296 --> 00:01:06,200
This thick insulation protects against temperature extremes and radiation.

17
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:11,073
Next is what they call a "bladder" of oxygen --- that's an inflatable sac,

18
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:13,697
filled with oxygen, to simulate atmospheric pressure.

19
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:20,070
This bladder presses against the body with the same force as the Earth's atmosphere at sea level.

20
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:23,548
The innermost layers provide liquid cooling and ventilation.

21
00:01:23,548 --> 00:01:30,070
Despite all the layers, the suit is flexible, allowing free movement so we can work.

22
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:33,455
Another really sophisticated part of the space suit is the helmet.

23
00:01:33,455 --> 00:01:38,070
I brought one along to show you. Can I have a volunteer come and demonstrate?
